umbency": 0.0, "money": -1.19}, "rating_margin": -8.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.16, "baseline_margin": -3.89, "analyst_adjustment": 0.89, "key_factors": ["R+7 district lean in an open seat after Amodei's retirement", "Flippo's Bronze Star misrepresentation scandal reported by the Washington Post", "Thin, older polling showing a near-tied race, including D-sponsored GBAO", "Flippo's large fundraising and outside spending edge over Benitez-Thompson"], "rationale": "The district's R+7 lean, Flippo's money advantage, and a Likely R expert consensus keep the Republican favored, but the Bronze Star scandal, a near-tied polling picture, and markets pricing the Democrat near a quarter justify moving a few points toward the Democrat from the fundamentals.", "watch": "Whether the military-record story damages Flippo with the district's veteran-heavy electorate, or fades, could shift this toward a genuine tossup or back toward Likely R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mark Amodei (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Mark Amodei's retirement opens a Republican-leaning northern Nevada seat that has rarely been competitive. Republican David Flippo, a Trump-backed veteran, has vastly out-raised Democrat Teresa Benitez-Thompson and drawn outside support, though he has spent most of it. Expert raters mostly call the race Likely Republican, with a couple at Lean R and one at Tossup.\n\nThe race has grown more interesting because of reporting that Flippo exaggerated his Bronze Star award, and limited polling shows a close contest. Prediction markets still favor the Republican by roughly three to one. The seat's underlying lean and the money gap keep the Republican ahead, but the scandal gives the Democrat a plausible opening in a low-turnout open-seat environment.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.213, "margin": -4.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2977, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2868, "margin": -4.0}, {"date